{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-48040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980753/assets/59c02273515e87fcebb8eb1785dc2cf0c6490feda4fc9644df43b8849921eec5.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980753/state.json" "file_sha256" "a5cfd4502c036ab527ad66086b792c0611ac2b932f6a2b41f6890842147e205f"} {"question" "q-48040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$ and we have $f(-1)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, both left-hand side and right-hand side limits exist but they are not the same. Therefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function contains a break exactly at $x=-1$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980753/state.json" "file_sha256" "a5cfd4502c036ab527ad66086b792c0611ac2b932f6a2b41f6890842147e205f"} {"question" "q-48040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980753/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1f0fe659e26fd8a6898c8e4a384044d988b015cc1e9d1eb222fceb862d48a0fc"} {"question" "q-48040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category